# Changelog

Notable changes to Tupista. Format follows [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com);
versioning follows [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org).

Entries here describe what changed for someone USING Tupista — internal
refactors belong in the git history, not in this file.

## [1.0.0] - 2026-08-30

First release: a desktop Sudoku assistant that explains its hints instead of
just handing over answers.

### Added

**Hints that show their working.** `nextHint` returns one next human step
together with the chain of eliminations that makes it true from what you can
actually see on your own board — never "naked single at R9C5" when your pencil
marks still show three candidates there. The chain is pruned to the deductions
the placement genuinely rests on, and is revealed in three stages: where to
look, then the mechanism, then the answer. The full step is never shown
unprompted.

**A human technique ladder, tiers 1-7.** Singles, pointing and claiming, naked
and hidden pairs and triples, X-Wing, XY-Wing and XY-Chain. Difficulty is
reported as the hardest tier a puzzle required; puzzles beyond the ladder say
so rather than guessing.

**Validation before advice.** Every hint checks the grid first. A board with
duplicate digits reports exactly which cells clash, and one without a single
unique solution says so — because a hint about an ambiguous grid would be
guesswork.

**Pencil marks and a mark audit.** Note candidates with the Pencil toggle or
Shift+digit, shown as a 3x3 mini-grid so each digit keeps a fixed position.
"Check my pencil marks" compares your notes against the board and flags the
ones that are missing, or that a later placement has quietly made stale. The
solver never reads your marks — a slip in them can't poison a hint.

**A desktop app.** Keyboard-first board entry with separate puzzle-entry and
playing modes, so the puzzle can't be edited by accident once you start.
Solve, difficulty rating, solution counting, and completion that announces
itself. Puzzles and preferences are saved to SQLite.

**Four colour schemes**, each with a light and a dark variant, chosen
independently in Settings and remembered between runs.

### Notes

The Sudoku engine is C++20 with no platform dependencies, reachable through a C
ABI, and is verified on GCC, Clang, Apple Clang and MSVC. That is groundwork
for an iOS version later; this release is Windows desktop only.
